<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">K'inich Janaab Pakal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/603">603</a>–<a href="/years/683">683</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Pakal the Great, Pakal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">K'inich Janaab Pakal was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a> from 615 to 683, the most celebrated ruler of the Classic Maya. He restored the city after sacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> and built much of the Palace and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-inscriptions">Temple of the Inscriptions</a>. His tomb inside that temple, with its carved sarcophagus lid and jade mask, was discovered by Alberto Ruz Lhuillier in 1952.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kinich-janaab-pakal.jpg" alt="K'inich Janaab Pakal" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergio; derivative work by TastyCakes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PacalII.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kʼinich Janaabʼ Pakal was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a> on 24 March 603 to Lady <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sak-k-uk">Sak Kʼukʼ</a> of the ruling dynasty and her husband Kʼan Moʼ Hix. His birth came in a turbulent time. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> had sacked Palenque four years earlier, and Calakmul's ruler Scroll Serpent led another catastrophic attack in 611. The following year the ruler Ajen Yohl Mat and his heir Janahb Pakal, the boy's maternal grandfather and namesake, both died, which created a crisis of succession. Pakal acceded in July 615 at the age of twelve, after an interim regency by his mother, who slowly ceded power over the years and died in September 640.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Pakal expanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a>'s power in the western part of the Maya lands and began a building programme at his capital. In March 626 he married Ix Tzʼakbu Ajaw, a descendant of the former Toktahn dynasty from Uxteʼkʼuh, and they had at least two sons, Kan Bahlam and Kʼan Joy Chitam. In 628 Piedras Negras captured one of his officials, and six days later Nuun Ujol Chaak, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajaw">ajaw</a> of Santa Elena, was taken to Palenque; Santa Elena became a tributary. He captured prisoners from Pipaʼ and Santa Elena in 659 and 663.</p><p>His first construction project, in 647, was the temple now called El Olvidado. He greatly enlarged the Palace, adding Building E, called the White Skin House, and Houses B and C in 661 and House A in 668, which is covered with frescoes of prisoners captured in 662. His wife died in November 672, after 47 years of marriage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pakal died in August 683 aged 80, having ruled for 68 years and 33 days. He was deified as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a>'s patron gods, and his sons Kan Bahlam and Kʼan Joy Chitam each became ruler in turn.</p><p>He was buried in a sarcophagus within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-inscriptions">Temple of the Inscriptions</a>. The archaeologist Alberto Ruz Lhuillier discovered the way into the tomb in 1948, and after four years of clearing rubble from the stairway it was uncovered in 1952. The skeleton lay with a jade mask and bead necklaces, among stucco reliefs of his passage to divinity.</p><p>The slight wear on its teeth led Ruz to doubt that the bones were Pakal's, but later analysis of the skeleton showed that the person died in their eighth or ninth decade, in agreement with the inscriptions. The carved sarcophagus lid shows him beneath a world tree, framed by the jaws of a funerary serpent and dressed as the Maize God. Archaeologists reject the claim, made by Erich von Däniken in 1968, that it depicts an astronaut.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kʼinich Janaabʼ Pakal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dead kings were buried within temples raised over their tombs, like that of <b>K’inich Janaab Pakal</b> at Palenque, and honoured as ancestors.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>…title of holy lord at Palenque from 583 to 604, Sak K’uk' was regent there for her son <b>K’inich Janaab Pakal</b>, who took the throne at twelve in 615, and Lady Six Sky ruled Naranjo for her young son and was shown standing over captives like a warrior…</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li><li><blockquote>Palenque was sacked twice by the Kingdom of Kaan, in 599 and 611, but recovered under <b>K'inich Janaab Pakal</b>, who reigned from 615 to 683.</blockquote><a href="/state/palenque">Palenque</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palenque">Palenque</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.48,-92.05&amp;year=655" aria-label="Palenque on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2050 1515 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of K'inich Janaab Pakal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2500 1785h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2500 1785h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17.48,-92.05&amp;year=655">What was here in 655 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
